      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(mcp): bound HTTP response body reads at 1 MiB",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\nFixes an issue where MCP server responses with large bodies could exhaust process memory. The `ensureGlobalFetchResponse` function read `response.text()` without any size check — a misconfigured or malicious MCP server returning hundreds of megabytes would buffer the entire body in memory, risking OOM.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\nSingle-chokepoint fix at `ensureGlobalFetchResponse` — both callers in `buildManagedMcpResponse` converge through this function. Two-layer guard:\n\n1. **Content-Length pre-check** — rejects before `response.text()` when the header declares an oversized body (including non-numeric values like `\"abc\"` which produce `NaN`).\n2. **Post-read byte-length guard** — uses `Buffer.byteLength(text, \"utf8\")` for accurate byte measurement, catching oversized bodies that arrive without a Content-Length header.\n\nCap is **1 MiB**, matching the merged `CONVEX_BROKER_RESPONSE_MAX_BYTES` precedent in #98619.\n\n## User Impact\nMCP server responses larger than 1 MiB are rejected with a clear error message instead of silently consuming unbounded memory until the process crashes.\n\n## Evidence\n\n**Vitest:** 15/15 passed (10 existing + 5 new tests)\n```\n$ node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/agents/mcp-http-fetch.test.ts --run\nTest Files  1 passed (1)\n     Tests  15 passed (15)\n```\n\n**New tests:**\n- `rejects body-less responses whose Content-Length exceeds the 1 MiB cap`\n- `rejects body-less responses whose actual text exceeds the 1 MiB cap`\n- `accepts body-less responses within the 1 MiB cap`\n- `accepts responses with Content-Length exactly at the 1 MiB cap` (boundary)\n- `rejects responses with non-numeric Content-Length header`",
        "branch": "fix/mcp-http-fetch-bound-response",
